      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure! When you compute the Fourier transform, you are essentially looking at your signal from a different perspective—one that emphasizes its frequency content instead of time domain behavior. Think of it like breaking a glass to see its impact points (frequencies) rather than the sequence of its fragments (time). The Fourier transform reveals the signal's composition, making it easier to analyze or modify specific frequencies. By isolating frequencies, you can highlight particular features, filter unwanted noise, or even reconstruct the original signal by combining only desired frequency components. It's like altering and hearing better different notes in a musical instrument rather than just observing the performance in sequence. Hope that helps!",
